From fb61b746d0860391c631cb006af4c99907a7ffc5 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arne Juul <arnej@vespa.ai>
Date: Fri, 21 Aug 2026 21:38:47 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 5/7] Search custom kernel registries when checking for a CPU
 kernel

InferenceSession::TransformGraph handed InsertCastTransformer only the last
entry of GetKernelRegistriesByProviderType, which is the CPU EP's own registry,
discarding every custom registry the session had. The transformer decides
whether a CPU-assigned fp16 node has a kernel, so a node covered by an fp16
kernel a user registered through a custom registry looked kernel-less: it was
rewritten to fp32 and wrapped in casts, and the user's kernel never ran. The
same narrowing applied to the fp32 lookup that confirms a fallback exists, so a
fallback kernel living in a custom registry was not found either.

Before the previous commits this could only affect an isolated fp16 island;
IsFp16NodeOnCpuWithoutKernel widened the check to every CPU-assigned fp16 node,
which widens the exposure too.

Pass the whole registry list through instead and search it in the order
GetKernelRegistriesByProviderType returns -- custom registries first, the CPU EP
registry last -- so both lookups agree with the priority
KernelRegistryManager::SearchKernelRegistry applies when the kernel is looked up
for real at session initialization. Both TryFindKernel overloads key their
lookup on the provider type, so passing registries that also hold kernels for
other providers is safe.

The single-pointer constructor stays as a delegating overload; the tests and
compare_provider_test_utils keep using it.

Co-Authored-By: Claude Opus 5 (1M context) <noreply@anthropic.com>
